            <ArticleTitle>[Asthma and high altitude. Medical evaluation of an asthmatic at Aconaguas--6962 meters].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>65-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>High altitude with low oxygen pressure and subsequent low blood saturation is known to lead eventually to HACE (High Altitude Cerebral Edema) and HAPE (Hi.Alt.Pulmonary Edema) even in healthy well-trained alpinists. Asthma is usually well tolerated in higher altitudes but still might be refered to as a predisposal to HAPE unless treated. The author reports a case of a 45 y.o.male asthmatic of Caucasian origin followed up to 6962m (highest spot on American continent between Chile &amp; Argentina) with measurement of O2 saturation-B.P.-H.R-PEV&amp;Peak-Flow-.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
